---
source: crates/pitboard/src/main.rs
expression: every_help_typed()
---
$ pitboard --help
Switch Claude Code and Codex between your own accounts without signing out, and
see what each account has left

Usage: pitboard [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]

Commands:
  status       What is signed in, and how much each Claude Code and Codex
               account has left (the default)
  use          Switch a tool to an enrolled account
  enroll       Add an account: the one signed in now, or with --sign-in, another
               one
  rename       Change the label an account is enrolled under
  forget       Drop an account and its parked login
  watch        Switch Claude Code before the account in use runs out, and warm
               up accounts
  warm         Start an account's five-hour period early, so it resets sooner
  statusline   One line for Claude Code's status bar; reads its session JSON on
               stdin
  renew        Renew every parked login that is due, and nothing else
  schedule     Keep parked logins alive without running anything yourself
  vault        Where parked logins are kept: in the login keyring, or in 0600
               files
  log          What Pitboard has changed, and when
  doctor       Check that what Pitboard relies on still holds on this machine
  uninstall    Delete every parked login this Pitboard wrote, the daily renewal
               schedule, the watch service and Pitboard's own files
  stow         Put away the login Claude Code left in a file behind the store it
               keeps its login in, then delete the file
  repair       Ask the credential store what parked logins are here, and account
               for every one
  abandon      Give up on an interrupted switch that cannot be finished, keeping
               every login
  adopt        Take over a Pitboard directory another computer wrote, keeping
               the accounts
  completions  Print a shell completion script
  help         Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --json     Print JSON instead of text
  -h, --help     Print help
  -V, --version  Print version

Examples:
  pitboard enroll personal        Add the account Claude Code is signed in to
  pitboard enroll work --sign-in  Sign in to another account and add it
  pitboard use work               Switch Claude Code to work
$ pitboard status --help
What is signed in, and how much each Claude Code and Codex account has left (the
default)

Usage: pitboard status [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --json     Print JSON instead of text
      --offline  Answer from what was last measured, without asking anyone
      --fresh    Ask about every account, even one asked about moments ago
  -h, --help     Print help
$ pitboard use --help
Switch a tool to an enrolled account

Usage: pitboard use [OPTIONS] <LABEL>

Arguments:
  <LABEL>  The label the account was enrolled under, such as `work` or
           `codex/work`

Options:
      --json  Print JSON instead of text
  -y, --yes   Where the service refuses the login in use for good, put this
              account's login in its place without asking; needed without a
              terminal or with `--json`
  -h, --help  Print help
$ pitboard enroll --help
Add an account: the one signed in now, or with --sign-in, another one

Usage: pitboard enroll [OPTIONS] <LABEL>

Arguments:
  <LABEL>  A short name for this account, such as `personal` or `work`.
           `codex/work` names a Codex account; a bare name means Claude Code

Options:
      --json     Print JSON instead of text
      --sign-in  Sign in to another account first, through the tool's own
                 sign-in
  -h, --help     Print help
$ pitboard rename --help
Change the label an account is enrolled under

Usage: pitboard rename [OPTIONS] <FROM> <TO>

Arguments:
  <FROM>  The label it has now
  <TO>    The label it should have

Options:
      --json  Print JSON instead of text
  -h, --help  Print help
$ pitboard forget --help
Drop an account and its parked login

Usage: pitboard forget [OPTIONS] <LABEL>

Arguments:
  <LABEL>  The label to drop

Options:
      --json  Print JSON instead of text
  -y, --yes   Do not ask first; needed without a terminal or with `--json`
  -h, --help  Print help
$ pitboard watch --help
Switch Claude Code before the account in use runs out, and warm up accounts

Usage: pitboard watch [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]

Commands:
  install    Have this computer's own service manager run `pitboard watch` from
             now on and at each sign-in
  status     Say whether it is installed
  uninstall  Stop it and take it away
  help       Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --at <PERCENT>  Switch once a limit of the account in use reaches this
                      share, from 50 to 99 [default: 95]
      --json          Print JSON instead of text
      --once          Decide once whether to switch, from the usage Pitboard
                      last measured, asking no service for usage, and stop.
                      Warms nothing up
  -h, --help          Print help

Anthropic's terms may cover automatic switching and warm-ups:
https://docs.usepitboard.com/concepts/limits#is-this-allowed
$ pitboard watch install --help
Have this computer's own service manager run `pitboard watch` from now on and at
each sign-in

Usage: pitboard watch install [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --at <PERCENT>  Switch once a limit of the account in use reaches this
                      share, from 50 to 99 [default: 95]
      --json          Print JSON instead of text
  -h, --help          Print help
$ pitboard watch status --help
Say whether it is installed

Usage: pitboard watch status [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --json  Print JSON instead of text
  -h, --help  Print help
$ pitboard watch uninstall --help
Stop it and take it away

Usage: pitboard watch uninstall [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --json  Print JSON instead of text
  -h, --help  Print help
$ pitboard warm --help
Start an account's five-hour period early, so it resets sooner

Usage: pitboard warm [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]

Commands:
  now    Start an account's five-hour period now, whatever the span, through one
         short request Claude Code makes with that account's login
  on     Warm up an account from now on, within the span `range` sets
  off    Stop warming up an account
  range  Warm up accounts only between these times of each day, in this
         computer's time zone
  help   Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --json  Print JSON instead of text
  -h, --help  Print help
$ pitboard warm now --help
Start an account's five-hour period now, whatever the span, through one short
request Claude Code makes with that account's login

Usage: pitboard warm now [OPTIONS] <LABEL>

Arguments:
  <LABEL>  The label the account was enrolled under, such as `work`

Options:
      --json  Print JSON instead of text
  -h, --help  Print help
$ pitboard warm on --help
Warm up an account from now on, within the span `range` sets

Usage: pitboard warm on [OPTIONS] <LABEL>

Arguments:
  <LABEL>  The label the account was enrolled under, such as `work`

Options:
      --json  Print JSON instead of text
  -h, --help  Print help
$ pitboard warm off --help
Stop warming up an account

Usage: pitboard warm off [OPTIONS] <LABEL>

Arguments:
  <LABEL>  The label the account was enrolled under

Options:
      --json  Print JSON instead of text
  -h, --help  Print help
$ pitboard warm range --help
Warm up accounts only between these times of each day, in this computer's time
zone

Usage: pitboard warm range [OPTIONS] <FROM> <TO>

Arguments:
  <FROM>  When to start, such as 06:00
  <TO>    When to stop, such as 22:00; 00:00 and 24:00 are midnight, and a time
          before FROM runs past it

Options:
      --json  Print JSON instead of text
  -h, --help  Print help
